23
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.190
Yeah, I mean, I was kind of surprised

24
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.770
that it became like a thing afterwards,

25
00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.120
because when I think about the people

26
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.010
who are the biggest part of my life,

27
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.500 line:15%
who make it possible for me to work,

28
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.910 line:15%
it's my husband and my nanny.

29
00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.930 line:15%
She's just the coolest person in the world.

30
00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.850 line:15%
And she works so hard.

31
00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.560 line:15%
And how lucky am I that I have a person like that

32
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.670
taking care of my child so I can really go to work

33
00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.430
and concentrate and not be worried.

34
00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:28.560
I mean, what a fortunate thing.

35
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.700
Melanie, I really think you might have opened a new door

36
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.650
for all of us women to acknowledge people

37
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.560
in our inner circle in a different way.

38
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.451
And I thank you for that.

39
00:01:38.451 --> 00:01:39.769
It's big deal.
(audience applauds)

40
00:01:39.769 --> 00:01:42.691
Thank you so much for saying that. Thanks.

41
00:01:42.691 --> 00:01:43.524
I fully agree.

42
00:01:43.524 --> 00:01:45.740
I think what a beautiful thing to do

43
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:50.290
and there's no way my life, either,

44
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:53.210
would exist without the two wonderful nannies

45
00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.920 line:15%
that care of my beautiful kids.

46
00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:56.880 line:15%
So thank you, Melanie.

47
00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.240 line:15%
And thank you to everyone out there

48
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.160
who's making everyone else's life

49
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.470
possible outside of the house.

50
00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.270
We could not do it without the support teams

51
00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.920
that we have, impossible.

52
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.715
And we shouldn't be ashamed

53
00:02:07.715 --> 00:02:11.150
to tell the truth about that, I don't think.

54
00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.108
Well, it's funny,

55
00:02:12.108 --> 00:02:15.800
we do live in a different, more modern society.

56
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.023
And the show that you're doing,

57
00:02:19.254 --> 00:02:23.670
is a bit aesthetically of a period piece.

58
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.380
It's a true story.

59
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.090
You guys, this is one of the most eerie, suspenseful,

60
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.380
it gives my flesh tingles

61
00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.945
and makes it crawl in all the right ways.

62
00:02:34.945 --> 00:02:37.035
It's so good!
(Jessica laughs)

63
00:02:37.035 --> 00:02:38.470
How could you make a show this good?

64
00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:43.100
It's intimate, but it just, ah! Ah!

65
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.540
What was your approach to this show, Jessica?

66
00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:51.540
There was a real sense of importance in creating

67
00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.410
the mundanity of a suburban life,

68
00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:59.643
especially back in 1980 in a place like Wylie, Texas.

69
00:03:00.961 --> 00:03:04.950
And to sit in those very normal, somewhat eerie,

70
00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:11.690
strange, isolating experiences and to really see,

71
00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.780 line:15%
specifically these two women

72
00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.653 line:15%
in their separate, but similar,

73
00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.670 line:15%
their mindsets which are very different,

74
00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:26.563
but also I really believe going through very similar issues,

75
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:31.590
with feeling alone and feeling maybe a lot of regret

76
00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.760
and a lot of resentment about how their life has turned out.

77
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.513
And I think so many of us,

78
00:03:38.879 --> 00:03:41.650
we can really empathize and understand that feeling.

79
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:43.210
And there are choices made,

80
00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:46.640
like Melanie, when you're sitting in your house,

81
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.390
you're alone and the baby keeps crying,

82
00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.990
and it's like, there's no trickery.

83
00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:56.940
It's the onset of a baby crying repeatedly

84
00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.400
throughout the day and the night in different intervals.

85
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.010
And I'm like, this has so many layers to it.

86
00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.603
How did you do it?

87
00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:11.900
Well, I mean, I felt her very deeply in my soul.

88
00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.300
She was somebody who was a complicated person,

89
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.960
not the easiest person,

90
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:20.100 line:15%
and a few people that I talked to, believed

91
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.600 line:15%
that she had kind of undiagnosed postpartum depression

92
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.630 line:15%
because it wasn't really discussed in 1980

93
00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.630
and she was really struggling.

94
00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:32.070
It just moved me so much that she had no one to turn to.

95
00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:33.700
And it's part of the heartbreak of the story

96
00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.310
is 'cause she felt at a certain point

97
00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:37.320
like she could turn to Candy as a friend.

98
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.290
Like she had one friend finally

99
00:04:39.290 --> 00:04:41.468
and then-
Oh yeah, yeah, she could.

100
00:04:41.468 --> 00:04:44.051 line:15%
(upbeat music)

